## TestClock — deterministic virtual clock for async tests (spec §4.7). A
## `--test`-style mode where the per-thread reactor swaps its real OS timer for
## an in-memory virtual clock + ordered timer queue, so timeouts / retries /
## `Async.sleep` resolve INSTANTLY and deterministically — no real sleeps.
##
## SURFACE:
##   * `TestClock.install -> TestClock` — flips the current thread into virtual
##     mode (virtual-now anchored at the real monotonic now) and returns a GUARD
##     value. The guard's `def drop` calls `uninstall`, restoring the real clock
##     at scope exit — so a test's time-virtualization is scoped to the guard's
##     lifetime (RAII install/restore).
##   * `TestClock.advance(d: &Duration)` — jump virtual time forward by `d`,
##     firing every timer whose deadline is now reached + waking its parked task.
##   * `TestClock.advance_to_next -> Int` — jump to the EARLIEST pending timer's
##     deadline (firing it), returning the ns advanced; returns -1 if no timer
##     is pending. Lets a test step deterministically deadline-by-deadline.
##   * `TestClock.set(d: &Duration)` — set virtual-now to an ABSOLUTE instant
##     (`d` ns since the virtual epoch), firing every timer at/before it.
##
## WHICH TIME SEAMS ARE VIRTUAL (spec §4.7 Correction 1): BOTH the async sleep
## deadline (`ruxen_time_now_ns`, read by `TimeSleepFuture`) AND sync
## `Instant.now`/`Instant.elapsed` (which sample the monotonic clock
## INDEPENDENTLY) route through the same TestClock seam — so a test sees virtual
## time on both. Wall-clock `Time.unix_ns` (REALTIME) is NOT virtualized.
##
## HYBRID: only the timer + clock seam is virtualized; real epoll/kqueue fd
## readiness is untouched, so a test mixing real sockets + virtual time gets
## real I/O wakeups AND virtual timer wakeups (matches tokio's `pause()`).
##
## v1: SINGLE-THREAD (the cell is thread-local) — install the clock on the
## thread that drives `block_on`. NO compiler changes: this is a C-runtime
## seam + this `.rx` namespace/guard class. Spec: §4.7.
class TestClock
  active: Int

  def init
    self.active = 1
  end

  ## Install the virtual clock on this thread; returns a guard whose `def drop`
  ## restores the real clock.
  ##
  ## The guard is a fresh `TestClock` the caller owns, so binding it
  ## (`var clock = TestClock.install`) restores the real clock when the
  ## guard's scope ends, and a discarded guard (`TestClock.install` as a bare
  ## statement) restores it at the end of that statement. `uninstall` stays
  ## the explicit teardown; install/uninstall are NESTING-AWARE: only the
  ## outermost install anchors and the outermost uninstall restores.
  def self.install -> TestClock
    ruxen_test_clock_install()
    TestClock.new
  end

  ## Explicitly restore the real clock (the v1 teardown surface — see `install`).
  ## Nesting-aware: only the outermost uninstall restores.
  def self.uninstall -> nil
    ruxen_test_clock_uninstall()
    nil
  end

  ## Advance virtual time by `d`, firing every now-due timer.
  def self.advance(d: &Duration) -> nil
    ruxen_test_clock_advance(d.as_nanos())
    nil
  end

  ## Jump to the next pending timer's deadline (firing it); returns the ns
  ## advanced, or -1 if nothing is pending.
  def self.advance_to_next -> Int
    ruxen_test_clock_advance_to_next()
  end

  ## Set virtual-now to an absolute instant (`d` ns since the virtual epoch).
  def self.set(d: &Duration) -> nil
    ruxen_test_clock_set(d.as_nanos())
    nil
  end

  ## Guard restore: drop the install guard → restore the real clock.
  def drop
    ruxen_test_clock_uninstall()
  end
end

## §4.7 TestClock C surface (runtime/test_clock.c). Module-level lib decls so the
## class statics call them by bare identifier (the `ruxen_reactor_*` / scheduler
## decl convention).
lib "runtime/test_clock.c"
  def ruxen_test_clock_install -> nil
  def ruxen_test_clock_uninstall -> nil
  def ruxen_test_clock_advance(delta_ns: Int) -> nil
  def ruxen_test_clock_advance_to_next -> Int
  def ruxen_test_clock_set(absolute_ns: Int) -> nil
end

## §4.7 test-observability: the reactor's live registration count (excludes the
## background wake fd). A deregister-of-fired-virtual-timer pin reads it to prove
## no double-decrement strands `park_current`'s fast-path.
lib "runtime/reactor.c"
  def ruxen_reactor_registered_count -> Int
end
